// git fetch vs git pull

git fetch
 - Downloads commits, branches, and tags from the remote repository
 - Does NOT modify your working files or local branches
 - Updates only your remote-tracking branches (e.g., origin/main)
 - Safe to run anytime—no merge conflicts possible
 
Use when:
 - You want to see what’s changed on the remote before integrating it
 - You want to review changes, diff, or merge manually
 
git fetch
git diff origin/main
git merge origin/main


git pull
 - Performs git fetch + integration (merge or rebase)
 - Automatically updates your local branch with remote changes
 - Can modify your working directory
 - Can cause merge conflicts
 
Use when:
 - You’re ready to immediately bring your local branch up to date with the remote
 - You don’t need to inspect the changes first
 
Default behavior:
git pull = git fetch + git merge

Summary Table:
| Command     | Fetch changes? | Update local branch?  | Safe?        | Might cause conflicts? |
| ----------- | -------------- | --------------------- | ------------ | ---------------------- |
| 'git fetch' | ✔️ Yes         | ❌ No                  | ✔️ Very safe | ❌ No                  |
| 'git pull'  | ✔️ Yes         | ✔️ Yes (merge/rebase)  | ⚠️ Depends   | ✔️ Yes                 |
 